6. difference b/n t.start() and t.run()
| `t.start()`                                  | `t.run()`                                         |
| -------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| Creates a new thread                         | Does not create a new thread                      |
| `run()` executes in the newly created thread | `run()` executes in the current thread            |
| Enables **multithreading**                   | No multithreading                                 |
| JVM schedules the new thread                 | Normal method invocation                          |
| Can be executed only once for a thread       | Can be called like a normal method multiple times |

7. importance of start()
   start() is responsible for starting a new thread and performing the necessary JVM and OS-level operations to make the thread eligible for thread scheduling. It then causes the run() method to execute in the newly created thread. Therefore, simply calling run() cannot create a new thread.

   JVM creates a new thread.
   The new thread becomes eligible for execution.
   The thread scheduler decides when it gets CPU time.
   The new thread executes the appropriate run() method.

8. overloading of run()
   run() can be overloaded, but start() invokes only the no-argument run() method. Parameterized overloaded versions of run() will not be invoked automatically by start()

9. If we are not overriding run()
   If we don't override the run() method, the inherited Thread.run() method will be executed when we call start(). If no Runnable target was provided, the default Thread.run() has no implementation to execute, so the thread performs no task.

10. overriding of start()
    start() can be overridden, but it is generally not recommended. If we override start() and don't call super.start(), it behaves like a normal method call and no new thread is created. To actually start a new thread, the overridden method must eventually call super.start().

