    But → not guaranteed.

19. public final void Join() throws InterruptedException
    join() is used to make the current thread wait until another thread completes its execution. For example, if the main thread calls t.join(), the main thread waits until thread t finishes, and then continues its execution.
	class MyThread extends Thread {

    public void run() {
        for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
            System.out.println("Child Thread: " + i);
        }
    }
   }

   class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {

        MyThread t = new MyThread();
        t.start();

        t.join();

        System.out.println("Main Thread completed");
    }
   }
   ⭐
   Calling thread waits; joined thread completes.


20.waiting of Child thread until completing main()
   class MyThread extends Thread {

    private Thread mainThread;

    MyThread(Thread mainThread) {
        this.mainThread = mainThread;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        try {
            mainThread.join();   // Child waits for main thread
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }

        System.out.println("Child Thread completed");
    }
  }

 class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Thread mainThread = Thread.currentThread();

        MyThread t = new MyThread(mainThread);
        t.start();

        System.out.println("Main Thread executing...");
    }
 }
 ⭐ Interview answer
  If the main thread calls child.join() while the child thread calls mainThread.join(), both threads wait for each other indefinitely, resulting in a deadlock.
  A thread calling join() on itself causes it to wait indefinitely because the thread can never complete while it is waiting for its own completion.
  ⭐Remember
  A waits for B + B waits for A → classic deadlock
  A waits for A → self-join / indefinite waiting

