   One thread can interrupt another thread by calling the target thread's interrupt() method. It doesn't forcibly terminate the target thread; instead, it sets the interrupt status, and if the target is blocked in sleep(), wait(), or join(), it receives an InterruptedException.

23. what is synchronization?
    Synchronization is a mechanism in Java used to control concurrent access to shared resources. It ensures that only one thread at a time can execute a synchronized section for the same lock, thereby preventing race conditions and maintaining data consistency.
	Ex:-
	class Account {

    private int balance = 1000;

    public synchronized void withdraw(int amount) {
        if (balance >= amount) {
            balance = balance - amount;
        }
    }
   }
   Synchronization is internally implemented using an object's monitor lock. Every Java object can be associated with a monitor. When a thread enters a synchronized instance method or block, it must acquire the corresponding object's monitor lock. After completing the synchronized section, the thread releases the lock.

