50. what is ExecutorService?
    ExecutorService is an interface in the java.util.concurrent package that extends the Executor interface and provides additional features for managing and executing tasks.
	⭐
	Important methods:-
	a)execute():-
	⭐execute(Runnable command):-Used to execute a Runnable task.
	Ex:-
	executor.execute(() -> {
    System.out.println("Task running");
    });
	Does not return a result.
    Fire-and-forget style.

	b) submit()
	   1)submit(Runnable task)
	   Submits a Runnable and returns a Future<?>
	   Ex:-
	   Future<?> future = executor.submit(() -> {
         System.out.println("Processing");
       });
	   You can use the Future to check whether the task completed or cancel it.
	   future.get();
	   For a Runnable, get() returns null when completed successfully.

	   ⭐2) submit(Callable<T> task)
	   Used when the task needs to return a result.
	   Ex:-
	   Future<Integer> future = executor.submit(() -> {
        return 10 + 20;
       });

	   3)submit(Runnable task, T result)
         Submits a Runnable and associates a predefined result with it.
         String result = "SUCCESS";
         Future<String> future =
             executor.submit(() -> {
            System.out.println("Processing");
        }, result);

        System.out.println(future.get());

        future.get() returns "SUCCESS" after the task completes.

        Integer result = future.get();

    ⭐⭐⭐
    c) shutdown()
	Stop accepting new tasks, but allow already submitted tasks to complete.
	Example:

    Tasks already submitted
            ↓
     Continue running
            ↓
         Complete
            ↓
    Executor terminates
	It does not immediately stop running tasks.

    d) shutdownNow()
	Attempts to stop the executor immediately.
	Ex:-
	List<Runnable> tasks = executor.shutdownNow();
	⭐⭐⭐
	attempts to stop running tasks by interrupting their threads
    Prevents new tasks from being accepted
    Returns tasks that were submitted but never started

	e)isShutDown()
	Checks whether shutdown() or shutdownNow() has been called.
	executor.shutdown();
    System.out.println(executor.isShutdown()); // true
	⭐⭐⭐
	isShutdown() == true does NOT necessarily mean all tasks are finished.

	f)isTerminated()
	Checks whether the executor has completely terminated.
	Ex:-
	executor.shutdown();
    System.out.println(executor.isTerminated());

    It becomes true only after shutdown has been initiated and all tasks have completed.
	⭐⭐⭐
	isShutdown()
    → shutdown process initiated?

    isTerminated()
    → executor completely finished?

	g) awaitTermination()
	Used to wait for the executor to terminate for a specified amount of time
	executor.shutdown();

    if (executor.awaitTermination(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {
    System.out.println("All tasks completed");
    }
	Meaning:
    "Wait up to 10 seconds for the executor to completely terminate."
	⭐⭐⭐
	It returns:
    true  → terminated within the specified time
    false → timeout occurred before termination
    It can throw InterruptedException.
	⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
    h)invokeAll():-
	Used to submit multiple Callable tasks and wait for all of them to complete.
	List<Callable<Integer>> tasks = List.of(
    () -> 10,
    () -> 20,
    () -> 30
   );

   List<Future<Integer>> results =
        executor.invokeAll(tasks);

    i)invokeAny():-
	Used when you have multiple Callable tasks but only need the result of one successful task.
	Ex:-
	String result = executor.invokeAny(tasks);
	Task 1 → takes 5 seconds
    Task 2 → takes 2 seconds
    Task 3 → takes 3 seconds

    invokeAny()
       ↓
    Task 2 completes first
       ↓
    returns Task 2 result

