53. what is Future?
    Future is an interface used to represent the result of an asynchronous computation. It allows us to check whether the task is completed, retrieve the result, or cancel the task.
	Example:-
	ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);

    Future<Integer> future = executor.submit(() -> {
      return 10 + 20;
    });

    Integer result = future.get();  // 30
	⭐⭐⭐
	submit() starts the task asynchronously.
    Future<Integer> represents the pending result.
    future.get() waits until the task completes and returns the result.

	⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
	Main methods:
    get() → get the result
	Future.get() is a blocking call. It waits until the asynchronous task completes and the result is available.
    isDone() → check whether completed
    cancel() → cancel the task
    isCancelled() → check whether cancelled

54. what are Thread Pools ?
    A Thread Pool is a collection of pre-created and reusable threads that are used to execute multiple tasks.

55. different types of thread pools
    1)FixedThreadPool:- ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(5);
	Creates a fixed number of worker threads.
    If all threads are busy, new tasks wait in a queue.
    Good when you want to control the maximum number of concurrent tasks.
    ⭐⭐⭐
    Remember: Fixed = fixed number of threads.
    2)CachedThreadPool:- ExecutorService executor = Executors.newCachedThreadPool();
